<!-- cell: title-page id="3b22d940-86c4-4ab5-958c-5f2760c2ab91" title="The Forty-Fourth Lodger" -->

<!-- cell: markdown id="6be0b316-df47-4ffe-846e-0f28e829b402" -->

Mrs. Agnes Pell had let the back bedroom at 9 Garnet Street to forty-three lodgers since her husband died, and she had a rule for each of them: rent on Fridays, no cooking in the room, and conversation in the kitchen after supper, because a house that is never talked in begins to forget it is a house.

The forty-fourth lodger came in November, in the rain, with no luggage at all. He was a thin, courteous man in a dark coat who gave his name as Mr. Lark and paid a month in advance with coins so old she had to take them to the bank to be told what they were worth.

He did not eat. She put a plate in front of him every evening, shepherd's pie or a bit of fish, and every evening he thanked her and left it untouched. The kitchen grew cold when he sat in it. The hall clock, which had kept time since 1952, stopped whenever he passed it, and started again when he had gone up the stairs.

But he talked. That was the thing. He asked about her husband, Wilf, and about the shop they had kept, and about her sister in Leeds who no longer wrote. He listened as if every word were worth keeping. No one had listened to Agnes like that for eleven years.

On the first Friday in December, as she was clearing his plate, he asked her gently, "Are you ready, Mrs. Pell?"

She thought he meant the rent, and laughed, and said not yet.

He asked again the next Friday, and the one after that, always kindly, never pressing. She began to understand that he was not asking about the rent. She began, too, to notice how tired she was, how the stairs had become a mountain, how her own hands looked like paper in the lamplight.

On Christmas Eve she set two places as usual, and sat down across from him, and looked for a long time at the empty chair where Wilf used to sit.

"Are you ready, Mrs. Pell?"

"Yes," said Agnes. "I think I am."

The hall clock stopped.

It was the new year before the neighbours noticed the milk on the step. They found her in her chair at the kitchen table, two places set, her face peaceful. Upstairs the back bedroom was locked. When the police opened it, the dust on the bed and the floor lay thick and unbroken, as if no one had slept there for years.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="5cc65917-bbaf-4d3e-81c4-1569df37c3a1" title="Agnes Pell" words="0-57, 217-227, 374-389" -->

A widowed landlady who has let her back bedroom to forty-three lodgers and has had no one listen to her for eleven years.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="4967a99f-a80a-4251-843b-3435e3274976" title="Mr. Lark" words="72-113, 114-117, 261-275" -->

The forty-fourth lodger, who brings no luggage, pays in ancient coins, never eats and stops the clock when he passes. The story never says what he is.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="49d01f94-bf28-4c14-abbb-9d8690173b1a" title="9 Garnet Street" words="145-153, 390-395" -->

Agnes's boarding house, with its cold kitchen, stopped hall clock and locked back bedroom.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="66c6d927-0385-4ea7-b97d-ffc868418452" title="Loneliness" words="185-207, 314-343, 360-373" -->

Agnes's long solitude since her husband died: the sister who no longer writes, the empty chair, and the neighbours who notice only the milk.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="3c026912-36e6-4cf8-9fdf-786d5287cc14" title="Death" words="154-177, 228-248, 288-313, 356-359" -->

Mr. Lark is death come for Agnes. It is never named: it is in the stopped clock, the cold, the untouched food, and the question 'Are you ready?'

<!-- cell: plot id="98231fb5-9b61-43b6-ac78-ae33ab286f9c" title="The forty-fourth lodger" words="58-71, 114-117, 154-177, 228-248, 249-260, 276-287, 314-343, 344-355, 356-359, 374-421" -->

A strange lodger arrives, never eats, and keeps asking whether Agnes is ready; she comes to understand the question and, on Christmas Eve, says yes; she is found dead in her chair, and his room has not been slept in for years.
